<p>You are up a ladder. Phone rings. Your hands are full of plaster, or cable, or pipe fittings. You cannot answer. By the time you call back three hours later, they have already booked someone else.</p>

<p>This happens to tradies every single day. And it is not a minor inconvenience. It is the single biggest reason trades businesses lose work.</p>

<p>Your biggest competitor is not the next plumber on Google. It is the missed call. Research shows that <strong>85 percent of people whose calls go unanswered will not call back</strong>. They will just scroll to the next listing and tap that number instead. One missed call. One lost job. Multiply that across a week and you start to see the real cost.</p>

<p>The good news? You do not need to answer every call to keep every lead. You need a system that catches them for you. That is what a CRM does. And before you roll your eyes at another piece of software, stick around. This is not corporate nonsense. It is practical, tradie-tested stuff that takes about an afternoon to set up and saves you money from day one.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Why tradies lose leads (and it is not because the work is bad)</h2>

<p>Let us be honest. Most tradies are brilliant at the actual work. The problem is never the plumbing, the wiring, or the carpentry. The problem is everything around it. The admin. The follow-up. The quoting. The bits that happen between jobs.</p>

<p>Here is where leads typically go missing:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Missed calls while on the job.</strong> You cannot answer the phone when you are under a house or halfway through a cut. By the time you check your voicemail, the customer has moved on.</li>
  <li><strong>Quotes scribbled on scraps of paper.</strong> You price up a job on the back of a receipt, shove it in your pocket, and forget to send it. Or you send it and then never follow up to see if they want to go ahead.</li>
  <li><strong>No system for tracking jobs.</strong> Which quotes have you sent? Which ones are still waiting? Which jobs are booked? Which ones need invoicing? If the answer is "it is all in my head," you are guaranteed to be dropping things.</li>
  <li><strong>Client details scattered everywhere.</strong> Texts, emails, voicemails, Facebook messages, handwritten notes. Good luck finding that address the client sent you three weeks ago.</li>
  <li><strong>No automated follow-up.</strong> You quote a job, hear nothing back, and assume they went with someone else. Half the time they just got busy and forgot. A simple <a href="/blog/customer-follow-up-system">follow-up system</a> would have closed the deal.</li>
</ul>

<p>None of this means you are bad at your job. It means you do not have a system. And without a system, the busier you get, the more leads you lose. It is a painful cycle.</p>

