<h2>Setting up your job pipeline</h2>

<p>A job pipeline is just a visual way of tracking where every job sits. Think of it like a Kanban board, but for trade work. Each job moves through stages from left to right:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Enquiry.</strong> Someone has reached out. You know their name and what they need, but you have not quoted yet.</li>
  <li><strong>Quoted.</strong> You have sent a price. Now you are waiting on their response.</li>
  <li><strong>Booked.</strong> They said yes. The job is locked in and on your calendar.</li>
  <li><strong>In Progress.</strong> You are on site, doing the work.</li>
  <li><strong>Complete.</strong> The job is finished.</li>
  <li><strong>Paid.</strong> Invoice settled. Done and dusted.</li>
</ul>

<p>When everything lives in a pipeline, you never have to wonder what needs your attention. Open the app, look at the board, and you can see at a glance which quotes need sending, which clients have not responded, and which invoices are still outstanding.</p>

<p>No more guessing. No more sticky notes on the dash. No more "I think I quoted that bloke in Penrith but I cannot remember."</p>

<p>The pipeline also helps you spot bottlenecks. If you have got 15 jobs stuck in the "Quoted" column, that tells you your follow-up game needs work. If everything is piling up in "Complete" but not moving to "Paid," you have got an invoicing problem. The pipeline makes these patterns visible so you can fix them.</p>

<p>For more on how a <a href="/blog/crm-small-business">CRM for small business</a> uses pipelines to keep you organised, we have a full breakdown in our dedicated guide.</p>

<h2>Automated review requests (your Google ranking depends on this)</h2>

<p>Tradies live and die by their Google reviews. When someone searches "electrician near me" or "plumber Sydney," Google shows the businesses with the most reviews and the highest ratings first. A tradie with 200 five-star reviews will always outrank one with 15, even if the one with 15 does better work.</p>

<p>That is not fair, but it is how the algorithm works. And the tradies who understand this are cleaning up.</p>

<p>The problem is that asking for reviews feels awkward. You finish a job, the client is happy, and you think "I should ask for a review." But you are already running late to the next job, so you do not. And the moment passes.</p>

<p>A CRM fixes this by automating the ask. When you move a job to "Complete" in your pipeline, the system automatically sends the client an <a href="/blog/sms-marketing-small-business">SMS</a> with a direct link to your Google review page. Something like:</p>

<p><strong>"Thanks for choosing us for your [job type]. If you are happy with the work, we would really appreciate a quick Google review. Here is the link: [link]. Cheers!"</strong></p>

<p>No awkwardness. No forgetting. Just a consistent, polite request that goes out every single time. Over six months, this compounds into a serious competitive advantage. Our guide on <a href="/blog/get-more-google-reviews">getting more Google reviews</a> covers the full strategy, including what to do about negative reviews and how to respond to build trust.</p>

<blockquote><p>You do not need to be the cheapest tradie. You need to be the most reviewed. Google rewards consistency, and an automated review system gives you exactly that.</p></blockquote>

